form_description:
Here is the JSON schema, UI schema, and detailed explanation for defining Relationships in the C4 model.

This is the "glue" of the architecture. In C4, a relationship describes how one element (Source) connects to another (Target), the protocol used, and the direction of the flow.

1. The Relationship Schema
This schema captures the essential details needed to draw a line between two boxes: who is connecting, how they are connecting, and why.

JSON
{
  "title": "C4 Relationship Definition",
  "description": "Define a unidirectional connection between two architectural elements.",
  "type": "object",
  "required": [
    "sourceId",
    "targetId",
    "description",
    "technology"
  ],
  "properties": {
    "sourceId": {
      "type": "string",
      "title": "Source Element",
      "description": " The element initiating the action (e.g., 'Web App')."
    },
    "targetId": {
      "type": "string",
      "title": "Target Element",
      "description": "The element receiving the action (e.g., 'Database')."
    },
    "description": {
      "type": "string",
      "title": "Description (The 'Verb')",
      "description": "What happens? (e.g., 'Reads customer data from', 'Sends email via')."
    },
    "technology": {
      "type": "string",
      "title": "Protocol / Technology",
      "description": "e.g., HTTPS/JSON, JDBC, AMQP, gRPC"
    },
    "interactionType": {
      "type": "string",
      "title": "Interaction Style",
      "enum": [
        "Synchronous",
        "Asynchronous"
      ],
      "default": "Synchronous"
    },
    "tags": {
      "type": "array",
      "title": "Relationship Tags",
      "items": {
        "type": "string"
      },
      "uniqueItems": true
    }
  }
}
2. The UI Schema (UISchema)
This optimizes the form for selecting elements (assuming you populate the dropdowns dynamically) and clarifies the nature of the connection.

JSON
{
  "sourceId": {
    "ui:placeholder": "Select the source component..."
  },
  "targetId": {
    "ui:placeholder": "Select the target component..."
  },
  "description": {
    "ui:placeholder": "e.g., 'GET /api/v1/users'",
    "ui:help": "Keep it short. This text appears on the arrow line."
  },
  "interactionType": {
    "ui:widget": "radio",
    "ui:options": {
      "inline": true
    },
    "ui:help": "Synchronous = Solid Line. Asynchronous = Dashed Line."
  }
}
3. Explanation of the JSON Structure
A. The Core Fields (sourceId & targetId)
Purpose: These are the anchors of the relationship.

Implementation Note: In a real application, you would likely populate the enum or oneOf options for these fields dynamically using the list of components you created in the previous steps.

C4 Rule: Relationships are always unidirectional. If Component A talks to Component B and B talks back to A (independently), you typically define two separate relationships.

B. The "Verb" (description)
Purpose: This is the label that sits on top of the arrow in the diagram.

Best Practice: C4 advocates for specific descriptions. Instead of "Uses," say "Sends search query to." This schema enforces that capture.

C. The Protocol (technology)
Purpose: This describes how the data moves.

Diagram Impact: In Structurizr, this text usually appears in brackets [HTTPS] next to the description. It clarifies if the link is secure (HTTPS) or internal (TCP).

D. The Style (interactionType)
Purpose: Distinguishes between immediate blocking calls and background events.

Mapping to C4:

Synchronous: Maps to a Solid arrow. (The user waits for the page to load).

Asynchronous: Maps to a Dashed arrow. (The system sends an email in the background).
